diff --git a/docs/victoriametrics/vmagent.md b/docs/victoriametrics/vmagent.md
index 87a5b27..c713c07 100644
--- a/docs/victoriametrics/vmagent.md
+++ b/docs/victoriametrics/vmagent.md
@@ -967,6 +967,27 @@ to all the metrics scraped by the given `vmagent` instance:
 
 See also [how to shard data among multiple remote storage systems](#sharding-among-remote-storages).
 
+### Protecting against runaway target counts
+
+Service discovery sources such as cloud APIs, orchestrators, or service catalogs can occasionally return
+a much bigger number of targets than expected because of a misconfiguration on the discovery side
+(for example, an accidentally removed tag filter or an overly broad catalog query). Without any safety net,
+`vmagent` tries to scrape all of the discovered targets at once, which can spike CPU and memory usage and
+degrade scraping for every other job running on the same `vmagent` instance.
+
+The `max_targets_per_job` option at the [`scrape_config`](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs)
+level limits the number of active scrape targets a single `job_name` is allowed to have at once,
+regardless of which service discovery mechanism (or combination of mechanisms) produced the targets for that job.
+Once a job's discovered target count exceeds this limit, the extra targets are dropped instead of being scraped,
+so `vmagent` keeps running smoothly for the rest of the config. A global default for all jobs can be set via
+the `-promscrape.maxTargetsPerJob` command-line flag; a `job_name` may override this default with its own
+`max_targets_per_job` value. By default the limit is disabled (unlimited), so existing setups aren't affected.
+
+Targets dropped because of this limit are shown alongside other dropped targets at the `/targets` and
+`/service-discovery` pages (with the `targets limit` drop reason) and are counted in the `droppedTargets`
+section of the `/api/v1/targets` page, so it is easy to notice which job(s) are currently hitting the limit
+and roughly how many targets are being excluded because of it.
+
 ## High availability
 
 It is possible to run multiple **identically configured** `vmagent` instances or `vmagent`
